## Why This Is Asked
Designing runtime access control is critical for microservices in a ride-hailing platform. This tests integration of SPIFFE identities, policy as code (OPA), and Envoy ext_authz in a real-world, zero-trust setup.

## Key Concepts
- SPIFFE IDs and mTLS identity
- Open Policy Agent (Rego) for authz decisions
- Envoy ext_authz as a gatekeeper
- Service mesh zero-trust patterns
- CI test harness for policy validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal JavaScript test harness exercising OPA policy via REST API
const fetch = require('node-fetch');
async function test(spiffe_id, expected) {
  const input = { spiffe_id };
  const res = await fetch('http://opa.local/v1/data/authz/allow', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
    body: JSON.stringify({ input })
  });
  const json = await res.json();
  console.log(spiffe_id, json.result === expected);
}
(async () => { await test('spiffe://domain.org/finance/payment', true); await test('spiffe://domain.org/unknown', false); })();
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to apply attestation to WASM plugins in edge/k8s environments, a realistic gap beyond container images.

## Key Concepts

- WASM module signing with cosign
- Rekor attestations for wasm artifacts
- Runtime verification in a restricted loader
- Policy enforcement with Kyverno/OPA
- Edge CI/CD integration with registry labels

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo loader verification
async function verifyAndLoad(wasmName) {
  const att = await fetch(`https://rekor.example/attestations/${wasmName}`);
  if (!att?.valid) throw new Error('Attestation missing or invalid');
  // Load WASM module
  return await WebAssembly.instantiateStreaming(fetch(wasmName));
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests applying basic IaC security checks to Terraform in cloud contexts, ensuring encryption, least privilege, and network exposure are validated before deployment.

## Key Concepts
- IaC scanning with tfsec/checkov
- S3 default encryption (AES256/KMS)
- IAM least privilege
- RDS publicly accessible flag
- Local vs CI validation

## Code Example
```hcl
# Failing: S3 without encryption
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "bad" {
  bucket = "example-bucket"
}
```
```hcl
# Passing: S3 with encryption
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "good" {
  bucket = "example-bucket"
  server_side_encryption_configuration {
    rule {
      apply_server_side_encryption_by_default {
        sse_algorithm = "AES256"
      }
    }
  }
}
```
```hcl
# Failing: wildcard IAM actions
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "bad" {
  name = "perm"
  role = aws_iam_role.app.name
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [{ Action = "*", Effect = "Allow", Resource = "*" }]
  })
}
```
```hcl
# Passing: least-privilege IAM policy
resource "aws_iam_role_policy" "good" {
  name = "least"
  role = aws_iam_role.app.name
  policy = jsonencode({
    Version = "2012-10-17",
    Statement = [{ Action = ["ec2:DescribeInstances"], Effect = "Allow", Resource = "*" }]
  })
}
```
```hcl
# Failing: RDS publicly accessible
resource "aws_db_instance" "db" {
  allocated_storage = 20
  publicly_accessible = true
  engine = "mysql"
  # ...
}
```
```hcl
# Passing: RDS not publicly accessible
resource "aws_db_instance" "db" {
  allocated_storage = 20
  publicly_accessible = false
  engine = "mysql"
  # ...
}
```

